The result of <code>blockSum(expr, blocking)</code> Type the type of <code>sum(expr)</code> as a lattice field: a single-precision type becomes double precision (<code>LatticeComplexF</code> gives <code>LatticeComplexD</code>), other types stay the same Layout the layout of <code>expr</code>, blocked by <code>blocking</code>: <code>expr_layout.coarsen(blocking)</code> Shape the shape of <code>expr</code> Data format the default data format <p>The sums are computed at the precision of the result type, so a single-precision expression is summed in double precision.</p> <p>The layout of the result does not depend on the current layout. If the expression has leaves on several layouts, its layout is the finest one (see Mixed layouts). Since there is one layout per lattice size, the result is on the same layout as fields made in a scope of <code>fine.coarsen(blocking)</code> and can be combined with them:</p> <pre><code>LatticeLayout coarse = fine.coarsen(blocking);

auto r = blockSum(psi, blocking);      // r.layout() == coarse
{
LayoutScope s(coarse);
LatticeFermionD c = zero;
c += r;                              // the same layout
}
</code></pre> <p>Like every field computed from an expression, the result gets the default data format, not the format of the expression's leaves. Inside a <code>JitDataFormatScope</code> with half storage it is stored in half precision.</p>
